<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Makerere University</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1922">1922</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Makerere University in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kampala">Kampala</a> is Uganda's oldest and largest university. Founded in 1922 as a technical school, it became Makerere College, the leading centre of higher education in British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a>, a college of the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> from 1949 and part of the University of East Africa from 1963, before becoming an independent national university in 1970. Its alumni include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-nyerere">Julius Nyerere</a> and many other African leaders and writers.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/makerere-university.jpg" alt="Makerere University" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: OER Africa · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ssetendekero_Makerere.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The trade school that became Makerere began operating in 1921 with classes in carpentry, building construction and mechanics. In 1922 it was founded as the Uganda Technical College, with added courses in the arts, education, agriculture and medicine, and in the same year renamed Makerere College. In 1928 the vocational classes were separated and renamed Kampala Technical School. In 1937 the college began offering post-secondary certificate courses that awarded diplomas, not degrees. It is the oldest currently active university in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Asquith Commission, set up by the British government in 1943, recommended raising Makerere to full university level. It noted that in 1943 the student roll was still modest, with 114 resident students. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> government's proposal for a university led to a controversial struggle: the Bataka Party described it as a plot to steal African soil for European settlement, and rioting broke out in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kampala">Kampala</a>. In 1949 the college was granted university status, and in the same year the Bataka Party was banned after riot and arson.</p><p>Makerere became an independent national university in 1970. After Uganda's independence it was a focal point of the literary activity central to African nationalist culture, and writers including Nuruddin Farah, Ali Mazrui, David Rubadiri and Ngũgĩ wa Thiong'o were there at some point in their careers.</p><p>Student unrest and faculty disenchantment closed it three times between 2006 and 2016. After a strike over unpaid pay in 2016, President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoweri-museveni">Yoweri Museveni</a> closed it indefinitely, and it reopened in January 2017.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Makerere is the alma mater of many post-independence African leaders, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milton-obote">Milton Obote</a> of Uganda, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-nyerere">Julius Nyerere</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benjamin-mkapa">Benjamin Mkapa</a> of Tanzania, Joseph Kabila of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-republic-of-the-congo">Democratic Republic of the Congo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mwai-kibaki">Mwai Kibaki</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kenya">Kenya</a>. It also did much to develop primatology and wildlife field studies. In September 2020 a fire gutted its main building, the Ivory Tower, built in the 1930s and commissioned in 1941; an investigation could not establish the cause but pointed to an electric fault. Reconstruction of a building designed to look like the original began in April 2022. In 2022 the university marked the centenary of its establishment as Makerere College.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Makerere University” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Most Ugandans are Christians, with a sizeable Muslim minority, and <b>Makerere University</b> in Kampala remains a leading East African institution.</blockquote><a href="/empire/uganda">Uganda · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
